  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CDT, rain is starting to move north into eastern SD.\nThis is much later than expected from previous forecasts and\nwill affect rainfall totals. It also looks like the main body of\nthe precipitation will be farther east than expected, further\naffecting totals for areas along and west of the Sisseton \nHills/Prairie Coteau. Temperatures across the area are in the \nupper 50s to low 60s with winds out of the southeast at \ngenerally less than 10 mph. \n\nOur next chance for precipitation will be Monday through Tuesday as \na low moves southwest to northeast across the Dakotas. Precipitation \namounts are expected to be higher with this event than our rain \ntoday. Chance of more than half an inch are around 70-90% between \nthe Missouri and James Rivers north of Hwy 14 through Tuesday \nmorning. This is when most of the precipitation will fall, rain will \nlinger across eastern SD into the afternoon with minor additional \naccumulations. This will be a more widespread event with \nprecipitation starting in central SD late Sunday night/early Monday \nmorning and moving east across the forecast area. Rain should come \nto an end over eastern SD Tuesday afternoon.\n\nTemperatures through the period will be right around to slightly \nabove average, particularly across central SD. There is a cooling \ntrend towards the end of the forecast, but its pretty slight and \nonly a few degrees below normal. Winds Monday afternoon south of Hwy \n14 and east of the James could gust up to 30 mph, locally higher in \nsouth central SD. Other than this, winds look normal through the 7 \nday forecast.",
